# `optim::SeptimaExitFlag`

Why a run of `septima` stopped.

Only `Converged` (1) means a stopping test was met. `MaxIterations` (0) means the budget ran out. `Stopped` (−1) is a progress hook returning `true`. `NoProgress` (−2) means `max_no_progress_iterations` passed in a row without the objective improving. `NonFiniteEvaluation` (−3) means the objective, gradient or a constraint was NaN or infinity and recovery failed. `InvalidProblem` (−4) means the problem was inconsistent, for example a bound of the wrong shape.

## Fields

### `Converged`

`enumerator`, default `1`.

A stopping test was met.

### `MaxIterations`

`enumerator`, default `0`.

The iteration budget ran out.

### `Stopped`

`enumerator`, default `-1`.

A progress hook returned `true`.

### `NoProgress`

`enumerator`, default `-2`.

The objective stopped improving.

### `NonFiniteEvaluation`

`enumerator`, default `-3`.

NaN or infinity where recovery failed.

### `InvalidProblem`

`enumerator`, default `-4`.

An inconsistent problem.

## Guidance

Read as `SeptimaResult::exit_flag`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Converged and out-of-iterations runs.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto objective = [](const Series &x) { return (x - 3.0).square().sum(); };

  optim::SeptimaSolveOptions options, short_run;
  options.step.optimizer = optim::SeptimaOptimizer::Lbfgs;
  short_run.max_iterations = 2;
  out.writeln("L-BFGS    :", optim::septima_exit_flag_name(optim::septima(objective, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), options).exit_flag));
  out.writeln("2 iterations:", optim::septima_exit_flag_name(optim::septima(objective, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), short_run).exit_flag));
}
```

### Example 2

Stopped by a hook. The progress hook returns `true` to stop and `false` to keep going, as in `optima`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto objective = [](const Series &x) { return (x - 3.0).square().sum(); };

  optim::SeptimaSolveOptions options;
  options.progress_hook = [](const optim::SeptimaProgressSnapshot &s) { return s.iteration >= 3; };
  const optim::SeptimaResult result = optim::septima(objective, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), options);
  out.writeln("exit =", optim::septima_exit_flag_name(result.exit_flag), "=", static_cast<int>(result.exit_flag), ", iterations =", result.iteration);
}
```

### Example 3

A non-finite objective. `log` of a negative number is NaN and the run reports it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  optim::SeptimaSolveOptions options;
  const optim::SeptimaResult result = optim::septima([](const Series &x) { return x.log().sum(); }, pluss::series::make({-1.0}), options);
  out.writeln("exit =", optim::septima_exit_flag_name(result.exit_flag));
}
```

